I’ll first inspect the measurement metadata, pattern range/resolution, and the reusable refinement utilities in `learned/`. Then I’ll build a chemically screened phase inventory and refine it in GSAS-II, using residual-led additions only after establishing a stable baseline.

The chemistry points first to nickel tungstate, but the unknown stoichiometry and phase count make residual NiO/WO₃ and alternate Ni–W–O compounds genuine candidates. I’m now extracting peak positions and querying the local crystallographic index before committing to any structure record.

The strongest families separate cleanly into wolframite-type NiWO₄ (notably 19.19°, 30.87°, 36.52°, 54.58°) and monoclinic WO₃ (the 23.06/23.53/24.28° cluster plus 26.53°). I’m checking all locally available WO₃ polymorph records and keeping NiO on the watch list; its diagnostic 43.3° family appears absent or very weak.

