The common profile is stable and replayable, and refining the previously unknown polarization gives a physically plausible 0.720 and improves Rwp to ~5.02%. I’m now comparing the exact but incomplete 1951 NiWO₄ record against the high-quality ZnWO₄ prototype with Ni substitution, and separately challenging monoclinic WO₃ against its triclinic/orthorhombic alternatives under the same nuisance model.

The phase-by-phase profile tests are decisive: NiWO₄ size and strain both run to their neutral bounds, while WO₃ gains a replay-stable isotropic microstrain term (1438 ± 97 µstrain) and lowers Rwp to ~4.95%. I’m retaining only that term and now checking asymmetry and texture; unsupported terms will remain fixed at neutral values.

The structural refinement is now physically coherent: the coarse NiWO₄ coordinates refine to plausible octahedra (Ni–O 1.96–2.01 Å; W–O 1.90–2.21 Å), and the final nonnegative U/W/X profile is stable. NiO re-challenges to a scale floor with a ~0.09 wt% one-sigma detection estimate, so it will not be included. I’m pruning the rejected terms, replaying from a reopened project, and exporting all deliverables directly from that exact project state.

The final two-phase refinement has converged. I’m now checking that every exported CIF, fraction, and profile matches the reopened `final.gpx` exactly, then I’ll write the uncertainty-focused report.

The reopened project and all exports agree byte-for-value at the numerical level: 8,282 profile rows, both fractions, and every fit statistic match. The CIFs also parse cleanly as P2/c NiWO₄ and P2₁/c WO₃ with chemically sensible coordination and bond-valence states.

The remaining model ambiguity is now quantified: an almost pseudosymmetric triclinic WO₃ alternative fits only marginally worse and shifts the phase fractions by about 0.65 wt%, while orthorhombic WO₃ is clearly rejected. I’ll report the formal covariance errors separately from a more realistic ±1 wt% practical uncertainty.

